Dimensions of $\left(\frac{a b}{V^{2}}\right)$ are shown below.

\begin{aligned}
\frac{a}{V^{2}} \times b &=\text { pressure } \times \text { volume } \\
&=\frac{\text { force }}{\text { area }} \times \text { volume } \\
&=\frac{\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}}{\mathrm{L}^{2}} \times \mathrm{L}^{3}=\mathrm{ML}^{2} \mathrm{T}^{-2}
\end{aligned}

The units of energy (joule) is,

1 \mathrm{J}=1 \mathrm{kg} \mathrm{m}^{2} \mathrm{s}^{-2}=\mathrm{ML}^{2} \mathrm{T}^{-2}

Therefore, the dimensions of $\left(\frac{a b}{V^{2}}\right)$ is same as that of energy. So, the option (b) is correct.

From the formula, Surface tension, $(\gamma)$
=\frac{\Delta W}{\Delta A}=\frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{m}^{2}}=\frac{\mathrm{kg} \mathrm{m}^{2} \mathrm{s}^{-2}}{\mathrm{m}^{2}}=\mathrm{kg} \mathrm{s}^{-2}

Form the formula, $F=\eta \cdot A \cdot \frac{d V}{d x}$ or, $\eta$ (coefficient of viscosity)

=\frac{F}{A \cdot \frac{d V}{d x}}=\frac{\mathrm{N}}{\mathrm{m}^{2} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{ms}^{-1}}{\mathrm{m}}}=\mathrm{Nm}^{-2} \cdot \mathrm{s}

or $\mathrm{kg} \mathrm{ms}^{-2} \cdot \mathrm{m}^{-2} \cdot \mathrm{s}=\mathrm{kg} \mathrm{m}^{-1} \mathrm{s}^{-1}$

According to Graham's law of diffusion.
Rate of diffusion, $r \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{M}}=\frac{V}{t}$
Here, $M=$ molecular mass, $V=$ volume and $t=$ timel Thus, for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas,

\frac{200}{30}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}

for $O_{2}$ gas,

\frac{50}{t}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{32}}

Eqs. (i) and (ii), we get From

\begin{aligned}
\frac{200 \times t}{30 \times 50} &=\frac{\sqrt{32}}{\sqrt{2}} \\
t &=\frac{\sqrt{16} \times 30 \times 50}{200} \\
&=\frac{4 \times 30}{4}=30 \mathrm{min}
\end{aligned}

